<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ljubljana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Laibach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ljubljana is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, on the Ljubljanica river. The Roman colony of Emona stood on its site, and as Laibach it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. It served as the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> from 1809 to 1813 and hosted the Congress of Laibach in 1821, and in 1991 it became the capital of independent Slovenia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ljubljana.jpg" alt="Ljubljana" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Milligan Sumo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ljubljana_Old_Town,_Slovenia_(Old_Camera)_(33286165680).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ljubljana lies in the Ljubljana Basin in Central Slovenia, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the Karst, along a trade route between the northern Adriatic Sea and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> region and north of the country's largest marsh. The city centre sits on the Ljubljanica river at 298 metres, and Ljubljana Castle stands on Castle Hill to the south. The city stretches over an alluvial plain, and earthquakes have repeatedly devastated it, notably in 1511 and 1895. It lies some 250 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, 124 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and 350 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Its symbol is the Ljubljana Dragon, which appears on the tower of the castle, on the coat of arms and on the Dragon Bridge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 2000 BCE people living in pile dwellings settled the Ljubljana Marsh. Its prehistoric pile dwellings and the oldest wooden wheel in the world are among the notable finds, and the remains have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since June 2011. The area later served as a transit point for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>, the Iapodes and, from the 3rd century BCE, a Celtic tribe, the Taurisci. Around 50 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a military encampment that became a permanent settlement called Iulia Aemona, held by the Legio XV Apollinaris. It housed 5,000 to 6,000 inhabitants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> destroyed it in 452 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a>'s orders, and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> did the same.</p><p>The city was first mentioned in the first half of the 12th century, and the German name Laibach is documented from 1144. It was the historical capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>, a Slovene-inhabited part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, and was under Habsburg rule from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until 1918. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> from 1809 to 1814. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it became the capital of the Socialist Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and in 1991 the capital of independent Slovenia. In the 19th century tension between German and Slovene residents shaped even the city's sport, and the Ljubljana Sokol gymnastic club, founded in 1863 and succeeded in 1868, served Slovene cultural and political aims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ljubljana is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a>, with about 300,000 inhabitants, and its cultural, educational, economic and political centre. After the earthquake of 1511 it was rebuilt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style on Italian, especially Venetian, models, and after that of 1895 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-secession">Vienna Secession</a> style. The architect Jože Plečnik shaped large parts of the inter-war city, and Tivoli City Park, laid out in 1813 during the French administration, was renovated by him between 1921 and 1939.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ljubljana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Belgrade, Zagreb and <b>Ljubljana</b> nonetheless grew as modern cities, and writers such as Ivo Andrić and Miroslav Krleža and the sculptor Ivan Meštrović were active in the period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1905 a large crowd sang it at the unveiling of the monument to the Slovene poet France Prešeren in <b>Ljubljana</b>, and in the First World War Slavic soldiers on opposite sides of the front sometimes sang it to one another to show common feeling and avoid bloodshed.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a></li><li><blockquote>A governor-general in <b>Ljubljana</b>, first Marshal Auguste de Marmont, ruled under French law, abolishing many feudal dues, building roads and opening schools teaching in Slovene and Croatian.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom was mainly an administrative label: it was divided into two governorates, at <b>Laibach</b> and Trieste, each run by Austrian officials under the system of Metternich, with provincial estates of little power.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-illyria">Kingdom of Illyria</a></li></ul></section>
